      "heading": "The Whole System in One Picture",
      "html": "<p>Put the two halves together. The refrigerant half is a loop: compressor, condenser, receiver-drier or accumulator, expansion device, evaporator, and back. The air half is a path: filter, blower, heater box, ducts, vents. The two halves meet at the evaporator and the heater core, where refrigerant and air trade heat. Every complaint in this course lives in one of those halves, and the first diagnostic question is always: which half?</p>\n<p>No cooling with a compressor that never engages is an electrical or control fault. No cooling with a compressor that runs is a refrigerant or airflow fault. No heat is a coolant or control fault. Weak airflow is an air-path fault. Naming the half first stops the most common error in this trade: fixing the wrong half.</p>\n"
